  ],
  "text": "At night, your brain cells shrink so that fluid can wash between them. Everything that burns fuel makes waste, and your brain burns more than anything else you own. The rest of your body has a drainage system for that. Your brain does not have the same one. So it runs its own instead, and it runs it mostly at night. Brain cells contract, the gaps between them open up, and fluid floods through. It rinses out the day's waste, including proteins linked to Alzheimer's. And during deep sleep, that clearance runs far harder than it ever does while you are awake. Which means sleep is not the brain switching off. It is scheduled maintenance the brain cannot run while you are using it. Skip it, and nobody comes in to clean. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
